from pathlib import Path
import json, html
root=Path('assets/branding/sharpie/approved')
m=json.loads((root/'provenance/measurements.json').read_text())
labels={'primary':'Primary head','primary-badge':'Primary circular badge','smile':'Smiling head','minimal':'Minimal sitting mascot'}
def figure(key):
 return f'<figure><div class="art"><img src="svg/sharpie-{key}.svg" alt="Sharpie {labels[key]}"></div><figcaption>{labels[key]}</figcaption></figure>'
rows=''
for v in m['variants']:
 b=v['rendered_alpha_bounds_512']; w=b[2]-b[0]; h=b[3]-b[1]
 rows+=f'<tr><td>{labels[v["name"].replace("sharpie-","")]}</td><td>{", ".join(str(x) for x in b)}</td><td>{w:g} × {h:g}</td><td>{m["minimum_sizes_recommendation_px"][v["name"]]} px</td></tr>'
feature_rows=''
for v in m['variants']:
 for key,f in v.get('features',{}).items():
  vals=' / '.join(f'{x:.2f}' for x in f['bbox_xywh'])
  feature_rows+=f'<tr><td>{labels[v["name"].replace("sharpie-","")]}</td><td>{key}</td><td>{vals}</td></tr>'
body='''<header><p class="eyebrow">SHARPMUSH / IDENTITY STANDARD / 1.0</p><h1>Sharpie</h1><p class="lead">Mascot design and production standard</p><p>16 October 2026 · Approved selections, reconstructed vector masters</p></header>
<section><h2>01 / The identity</h2><p>Sharpie is a clever, quick feline familiar who builds words. The character is approachable and capable: alert tufted ears, a confident small smile, mint eyes and a distinct angular M on the forehead. Personality comes from attention and purposeful action.</p><div class="grid">'''+''.join(figure(x) for x in labels)+'''</div><p class="note">The owner selected three designs: the primary head, smiling head and minimal sitting mascot. The circular badge derives from the primary. The supplied SVGs are flat-color vector reconstructions of those selections, with pure-white face areas, refined Bézier contours and corrected primary chin. The rejected independent rounded design is removed; its replacement reuses the primary artwork on a white circle. The reconstruction establishes a reproducible working master; it does not claim pixel-identical recovery of original vector artwork.</p></section>
<section><h2>02 / Approved marks and roles</h2><table><tr><th>Mark</th><th>Selection</th><th>Use</th></tr><tr><td>Primary head</td><td>First sheet, option 1</td><td>Main mascot signature, website illustration, prominent brand presence.</td></tr><tr><td>Primary circular badge</td><td>Derived from first sheet, option 1</td><td>Avatar and circular-profile placements. Exact primary geometry on a white circular backing.</td></tr><tr><td>Smiling head</td><td>Second sheet, option 7</td><td>Small friendly reaction, welcome and success acknowledgment.</td></tr><tr><td>Minimal sitting mascot</td><td>Second sheet, option 8</td><td>Compact whole-character mark and restrained interface illustration.</td></tr></table><p>The first sheet’s original option 8 was rejected during refinement and is not a production asset. These roles are the production convention for this package. Keep the original SharpMUSH M emblem available as the separate project mark. No application assets have been replaced. Earlier full-body, anime and anthro studies are exploratory; their inclusion in the concept folders does not make them approved identity masters.</p></section>
<section><h2>03 / Color system</h2><div class="swatches"><div style="background:#17333E;color:white">Ink<br><strong>#17333E</strong><br>RGB 23 / 51 / 62</div><div style="background:#00DDB3;color:#17333E">Mascot mint<br><strong>#00DDB3</strong><br>RGB 0 / 221 / 179</div><div style="background:white;color:#17333E;border:1px solid #ccd6d9">White<br><strong>#FFFFFF</strong><br>RGB 255 / 255 / 255</div></div><p>Use exactly these three opaque fills for the mascot. White muzzle, cheeks, ear interiors, paws and highlights are solid white, never beige and never transparency. Transparent canvas around the silhouette is intentional. Dark teal defines fur and line structure; mint identifies the M, eyes and selected accents.</p><p>The original project emblem uses <code>#00F5B7</code>. Preserve that legacy source independently. The mascot working palette uses <code>#00DDB3</code>, normalized from the selected primary concept; do not silently mix the two mints within one mascot asset. Use sRGB for web delivery. Derive print separations with the printer’s actual ICC profile, rather than assigning an untested CMYK recipe.</p><div class="contrast"><img src="sharpie-approved-preview.png" alt="All four marks shown on neutral light and dark backgrounds"></div></section>
<section><h2>04 / Geometry and measurements</h2><p>All master SVGs use <code>viewBox="0 0 512 512"</code>. One design unit is one unit of this coordinate system. Scale uniformly; the master paths define the precise curves and angles. The current masters normalize paired details using mirrored geometry; do not apply further automatic symmetry or independently stretch the ears, M, eyes or muzzle.</p><table><tr><th>Mark</th><th>Visible bounds x₁, y₁, x₂, y₂</th><th>Visible width × height</th><th>Starting minimum canvas size</th></tr>'''+rows+'''</table><p class="note">Bounds are measured from 2048-pixel transparent renders before the delivery PNGs are downsampled to 1024 pixels and expressed in 512-unit coordinates, so the table has 0.25-unit sampling precision. Canvas size is not visible-art width. The source of record is <code>provenance/measurements.json</code>, which also contains SVG hashes and source crop coordinates.</p><div class="geometry"><svg viewBox="0 0 640 640" aria-label="Primary logo construction and clear-space example"><rect width="640" height="640" fill="#e9eef0"/><rect x="64" y="64" width="512" height="512" fill="white" stroke="#91a6ae" stroke-dasharray="6 6"/><image href="svg/sharpie-primary.svg" x="64" y="64" width="512" height="512"/><rect x="82" y="64.5" width="476" height="511.5" fill="none" stroke="#00a88a" stroke-dasharray="8 5"/><text x="320" y="30" text-anchor="middle" fill="#17333e" font-size="16">512-unit canvas · dashed green = 32-unit clear-space envelope</text><text x="320" y="620" text-anchor="middle" fill="#17333e" font-size="16">Uniform scale only · measure space from visible artwork</text></svg></div><p>Define <strong>u = 32 units</strong>, one sixteenth of the master canvas. Reserve at least u clear space outside the visible artwork on every side. For canvas display size S pixels, required clear space is S ÷ 16 pixels. At 128 px, reserve 8 px; at 64 px, reserve 4 px. Add external layout padding when the SVG’s internal padding does not supply enough space.</p><p>The primary circular badge’s disk is part of its visible artwork. The minimal mascot’s raised tail is part of its bounds. Do not center either variant by eye while ignoring those features. Center the complete 512-unit canvas, then make any placement adjustment at the layout level without modifying the master.</p><p>Starting minimums are proposed usage rules, not certified readability thresholds. Inspect the intended background at actual size and at 1× and 2× density. Below 24 px, use the established project emblem until a dedicated micro-mark has been designed and approved. Do not automatically squeeze the detailed primary into a favicon.</p></section>
<section><h2>05 / Character construction</h2><h3>Measured feature geometry</h3><p>Feature boxes are measured by Inkscape after SVG transforms, in 512-unit coordinates. Values are x / y / width / height, rounded to two decimals; full precision is retained in the manifest. They are bounds, not pupil centers. Native paths remain the authority for construction.</p><table><tr><th>Mark</th><th>Feature ID</th><th>x / y / w / h</th></tr>'''+feature_rows+'''</table><h3>Features that stay fixed</h3><ul><li>A mint angular M sits centrally on the dark forehead. Retain its two peaks and central notch; it must not become a V, random lightning streak or separate floating badge.</li><li>Pointed ears have tufted tips. Cream from older studies is now pure white. Preserve the selected mark’s ear shape and inner mint accents.</li><li>Cheek tufts frame a white muzzle. The primary’s mouth, muzzle and chin form a continuous face with no horizontal seam or detached chin patch.</li><li>The primary and circular-badge marks share the full rounded :3 muzzle: a triangular nose, short center stem and two curved smiling lobes. Do not replace this with a pinched descending mouth. Both marks use mint eyes with dark pupils and small white glints. The smile variant intentionally uses closed eyes; the minimal mascot uses simplified dark eyes.</li><li>The minimal mascot keeps its compact seated body, white paws, right-side raised tail and mint tail tip. Preserve this silhouette when using that approved asset.</li></ul><p>For new illustrations, derive colors and forehead marking from these masters and retain the feline identity. New poses and turnarounds need a dedicated model sheet; these head and sitting marks do not establish a complete back view, skeleton or limb-length specification. Do not invent those measurements and label them approved.</p><h3>Expression</h3><p>Use curious, attentive, calmly pleased and welcoming expressions. Keep smiles small and confident. The approved smile mark is a distinct asset; do not morph its proportions into the primary merely to make every face identical. Avoid angry brow cuts, exaggerated helplessness and frantic attention-seeking behavior in routine product use.</p></section>
<section><h2>06 / Placement and reproduction</h2><p>Use transparent PNG or SVG on quiet, solid backgrounds. Check that dark ear and cheek edges remain distinct. On dark surfaces where the ink silhouette disappears, place the unchanged mark on a light neutral tile with the prescribed clear space. Do not invert the palette or recolor the white face as a shortcut.</p><p>Pair the mark with live “SharpMUSH” text using the product’s existing type system. As a starting layout rule, leave 2u between visible art and text; the project has not approved a custom wordmark lockup. Keep lettering editable and separate from mascot paths.</p><table><tr><th>Use</th><th>Avoid</th></tr><tr><td>Uniform scaling and clear surrounding space</td><td>Stretching, skewing, rotating a static logo, clipping ear tips or tail</td></tr><tr><td>Provided SVGs for scalable graphics</td><td>Embedding a cropped concept sheet or raster image inside an SVG</td></tr><tr><td>Three controlled fills and transparent exterior</td><td>Beige faces, cyan-only badge accents, texture, gradients or added outlines</td></tr><tr><td>One appropriate mascot appearance with a purpose</td><td>Decorative repetition, obstructing forms or drawing attention away from reading</td></tr></table></section>
<section><h2>07 / Motion and banners</h2><p>Motion rules below define future production; this package contains static assets. Start with a blink, ear twitch, brief greeting and a letter-arranging wordcraft action. Use one action at a time and return to a stable resting pose. Preserve the forehead M, palette, ear-tip shape and face proportions during motion.</p><p>Suggested motion budget: blink 120–180 ms, ear twitch 200–350 ms, greeting 600–1000 ms, one-shot success acknowledgment 500–900 ms. These are art-direction starting points. Keep squash and stretch below about 5% of the resting silhouette; do not distort the master static logo to make frames. Build animation-specific eye and ear layers in a separate derivative with a clear link to its source master.</p><p>Prefer a short entrance followed by a still in reading-heavy interfaces. Supply a static fallback and honor reduced-motion settings. Ongoing decorative autoplay needs a pause, stop or hide mechanism. For banners, keep art and editable text separate, preserve clear space, and test both narrow and wide crops. Never crop the ears to force a layout. Create a dedicated alternate composition instead.</p></section>
<section><h2>08 / Asset delivery and maintenance</h2><p><code>svg/sharpie-{primary,primary-badge,smile,minimal}.svg</code> are the editable scalable masters. <code>png/</code> contains transparent 1024 × 1024 renders. The SVGs contain real filled paths and color groups, accessibility titles and descriptions, without raster image embeds or external font dependencies.</p><p>For decorative use, use an empty alternative text. When the mascot is the only label of a link, provide the destination’s accessible name, such as “SharpMUSH home,” rather than relying on the SVG’s title. When several SVGs are inlined in one DOM, prefix every ID and rewrite its references, including clip-path URLs and aria-labelledby tokens, to avoid collisions. Using separate img elements avoids shared-DOM ID collisions.</p><p>Reconstruction instructions, preserved approval sheets and hashes are in <code>provenance/</code> and <code>sources/</code>. Generate downstream files from these masters. For a revision, update the source process, rerender PNGs, refresh measured bounds and hashes, inspect both light and dark previews, and record the change. Never replace an approved master with an unreviewed concept generation.</p><p><strong>Preflight:</strong> confirm three-color palette, no cream fills, clean chin, correct forehead M, transparent exterior and solid white facial areas; check all marks at intended size; validate SVG XML and ensure no raster, scripts or external references; verify the source selection and delivery filenames.</p></section>
<footer>Sharpie / SharpMUSH · Identity standard 1.0 · Owner selections 16 October 2026 · Geometry and usage rules apply to the supplied reconstructed masters.</footer>'''
css='''*{box-sizing:border-box}body{margin:0;background:#e9eef0;color:#17333e;font-family:Inter,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:16px;line-height:1.65}main{max-width:1120px;margin:32px auto;background:#fff;padding:64px 72px;box-shadow:0 10px 50px #17333e12}header{padding:40px;background:#17333e;color:#fff;border-top:8px solid #00ddb3;margin-bottom:50px}.eyebrow{font-size:12px;letter-spacing:2px;color:#00ddb3}h1{font-size:76px;line-height:1;margin:22px 0}h2{font-size:26px;border-top:1px solid #d7e0e3;padding-top:26px;margin-top:48px}h3{font-size:19px}.lead{font-size:26px}p{margin:16px 0}.grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(4,1fr);gap:18px}figure{margin:0}.art{background:#f1f4f5;padding:12px;border-radius:12px}.art img{width:100%;display:block}figcaption{font-size:13px;margin-top:10px;font-weight:700}.note{font-size:14px;color:#4f6670}table{width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:14px;margin:24px 0}th{text-align:left;background:#17333e;color:#fff}td,th{padding:13px;vertical-align:top;border-bottom:1px solid #d7e0e3}code{font-family:monospace;background:#edf3f4;padding:2px 4px;overflow-wrap:anywhere}.swatches{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr);gap:16px}.swatches div{padding:25px;border-radius:8px}.contrast img{width:100%}.geometry{max-width:620px;margin:24px auto}.geometry svg{width:100%}footer{margin-top:50px;border-top:3px solid #00ddb3;padding-top:20px;font-size:12px}li{margin:8px 0}@media(max-width:720px){main{padding:24px;margin:0}h1{font-size:54px}.grid{grid-template-columns:repeat(2,1fr)}.swatches{grid-template-columns:1fr}table{font-size:12px}td,th{padding:8px}}@media print{body{background:#fff}main{max-width:none;margin:0;padding:0;box-shadow:none}header{print-color-adjust:exact}section{break-inside:auto}h2,h3{break-after:avoid}table,figure,.swatches,.geometry{break-inside:avoid}th,.swatches div{print-color-adjust:exact}a{color:inherit}}'''
b=m['variants'][0]['rendered_alpha_bounds_512']
body=body.replace('x="82" y="64.5" width="476" height="511.5"', f'x="{64+b[0]-32:g}" y="{64+b[1]-32:g}" width="{b[2]-b[0]+64:g}" height="{b[3]-b[1]+64:g}"')
(root/'sharpie-design-standard.html').write_text('<!doctype html><html lang="en"><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>Sharpie mascot design standard</title><style>'+css+'</style><main>'+body+'</main></html>')
(root/'README.md').write_text('# Sharpie approved mascot assets\n\n[Open the design standard](sharpie-design-standard.html).\n\nThree owner-selected designs: primary (sheet 1 option 1), smiling head (sheet 2 option 7), minimal sitting mascot (sheet 2 option 8), plus a circular badge derived directly from primary. The independently drawn rounded badge was rejected and removed.\n\nThe SVG masters and transparent PNGs use ink `#17333E`, mint `#00DDB3` and pure white `#FFFFFF`. The circular badge reuses primary geometry; the primary chin artifact is corrected and all contours are refined.\n\n- `svg/`: editable path SVG masters\n- `png/`: 1024 px transparent renders\n- `sharpie-approved-preview.png`: light/dark comparison\n- `provenance/`: reproducible reconstruction, measurements and hashes\n- `sources/`: preserved approval sheets\n\nThe professional HTML standard includes roles, measured bounds, palette, clear space, minimum-size recommendations, immutable features, expression, motion, banners, accessibility and asset preflight. It is self-contained within this directory and uses local asset references.\n')
print(root/'sharpie-design-standard.html')
